//? Title: Planar Quickhull: exact farthest decisions and actual candidate sets
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The convex hull keeps the extreme points of a cloud while discarding its
interior and collinear edge points. Planar Quickhull splits at lexicographic
endpoints, selects the farthest strict-left point and repeats on two child
baselines. Actual native candidate sets now support the method diagram, and
exact represented-coordinate predicates protect both side and farthest
decisions. The original twenty-point cloud remains the geometric example.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

For a fixed directed baseline \(a\to b\), signed area orders perpendicular
distance on its strict-left side. Choosing a maximizer \(c\) leaves only the
strict-left sets of \(a\to c\) and \(c\to b\); every other point is inside or on
the boundary of triangle \(abc\) and cannot contribute a new strict hull vertex
on that side. Inductively concatenating the child chains gives the hull.
$$ \Delta(a,b,p)-\Delta(a,b,q)=(b-a)\mathbin\times(p-q). $$
\alttext(The difference of two baseline areas is a determinant of two point differences.)
The new native area comparator evaluates this expression with exact dyadic
integer products, rather than subtracting rounded cross products. Side
predicates use the existing exact orientation API. This is exact for stored
finite binary64 coordinates, including subnormals and extreme ranges; it cannot
undo measurement rounding. Balanced splits often cost \(O(n\log n)\), but the
worst case is \(O(n^2)\). An iterative task stack preserves the recursive chain
order without risking call-stack overflow.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/quickhull_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/modular_exponentiation.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/integer_arithmetic.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <climits>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/number_theory.hpp>
#include <numeric>
#include <numbers>
#include <random>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <set>
#include <array>
#include <tuple>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/prufer.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/flow_diagram.hpp>
#include <datamunge/geometry/hull_algorithms.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/sampling_analysis.hpp>
#include <iomanip>
#include <bit>
#include <datamunge/geometry/quickhull.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    namespace h = dm::geometry;
    auto t = h::quickhull_trace({{0, 0}, {2, 0}, {1, 2}, {1, 1}});
    const auto &s = t.steps[0];
    std::print("\n| actual baseline table ids | farthest id | candidates | left child | right child |\n| " "--- | ---: | --- | --- | --- |\n");
    for (const auto &e : t.steps)
        std::print("| {} to {} | {} | `{}` | `{}` | `{}` |\n", e.a, e.b,
                   e.farthest, e.candidates, e.left, e.right);

    g::Diagram d;
    d.node("n0", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual first baseline ids\n{} to {}", s.a, s.b));
    d.node("n1", {10, 0},
           std::format("Actual exact farthest point\n({}, {})",
                       t.points[s.farthest].x, t.points[s.farthest].y));
    d.node("n2", {20, 0},
           std::format("Actual strict hull vertices\n{}", t.hull.size()));
    d.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    d.save_svg("build/quickhull_tiny.svg");
}
```
