> API and interpretation

The exact predicates replace the rounded cross and winding arithmetic only for
Quickhull; this does not claim a fresh robustness audit of Chan or other
geometry routines. Native histories index a sorted unique owned point table. The
original shape uses equal numeric and physical scales. Work counters count exact
predicate calls, not allocation, sorting comparisons, wall time or low-level
integer instructions. The square/parabola fixtures are controlled structural
inputs with seed42; their finite curves do not establish a universal
expected-time bound.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

